#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16432139-869b-5a5a-b784-83963542ab82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16432139-869b-5a5a-b784-83963542ab82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319456} :answer-field/id #uuid "573a3ae1-d186-53b0-8a09-1c50e55f3b28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c01baade-f79f-52bd-81ea-87c833e285e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-351911" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)={e}^{3x}$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{3x} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "608ccf0e-06c8-583a-a426-ff86893191a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "608ccf0e-06c8-583a-a426-ff86893191a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377550} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd4c2e8-f4ea-59cd-a7aa-2b954072bd85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d356ef5-8297-5b20-a0d7-9a9701f4028b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d356ef5-8297-5b20-a0d7-9a9701f4028b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377552} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df04968-76e1-52c2-910e-3ae80eed1d09" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd6991b-e069-5d1b-849b-1d9fcf52a45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd6991b-e069-5d1b-849b-1d9fcf52a45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377554} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5e0105-6833-5c05-ae89-a47bfd49c1d7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3194e767-6ac9-5deb-ab07-23b4411b5b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-351916" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ about $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,$ we plug $a=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3),$ ${f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =9 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =18 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3),$ ${f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =9+18(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2} \\\\  & =9+18(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+18{(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2619de-e2e4-5d96-81b3-945eaa80775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348568} {:answer/id #uuid "28e263a4-e9c1-5152-bc0b-70d80ade00cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348798} {:answer/id #uuid "37223aea-88bf-5c4a-b3d4-99196518ae2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{1/2}(1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348944} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3ec5f2-e3be-5be2-9c82-439f235b5142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348971} {:answer/id #uuid "a7737e7e-c654-5e57-8f8a-b76e619d290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7737e7e-c654-5e57-8f8a-b76e619d290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349938} :answer-field/id #uuid "7126cc2f-aeb2-5b19-a11e-65c74ef22d11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0dcf3f88-1f5e-5b29-b98b-e45db61b75ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-8269" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=(2-3x)^{3/2}$ about $x=0$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-3x)^{3/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =(2-0)^{3/2}={2}^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-3x)^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-0)^{1/2}=-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{27}{4}(2-3x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={2}^{3/2}+(-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2})x+(27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2})(\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94996d93-0d2b-5ad2-9e71-7de2292aee5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349776} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b85c93-400a-5fe3-adf5-f59817b3a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350165} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b16b34-3d73-5954-bff0-00e45b66cec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350347} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e1c382-deee-55db-8614-2ee9d7a5f7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350453} {:answer/id #uuid "f27d74ba-36a6-5d39-9284-008012a89045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94996d93-0d2b-5ad2-9e71-7de2292aee5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349776} :answer-field/id #uuid "15377768-284e-56d8-8d9d-946308b5a02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19f63370-b823-58a5-b86b-cdc73de5787a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83721" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x-3)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-3)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3) & =-\\frac{1}{(3)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-3)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{9})=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
